The cosine function is periodic, with period $2\pi$. This means that $\cos \theta = \cos (\theta + 2\pi k)$ for any integer $k$. We can use this fact to simplify the argument of the cosine function. Note also that $\frac{5\pi}{4} = \frac{\pi}{4} + \pi$, so we can use the trigonometric identity $\cos (\theta + \pi) = -\cos \theta$ to further simplify. Using All Students Take Calculus (ASTC), we know that $\cos \frac{\pi}{4}$ is positive because the sine function is squared away at the end, leaving only cosine. Therefore, we have:
\begin{align*}
\cos \frac{5\pi}{4} &= \cos \left( \frac{\pi}{4} + \pi \right)\\
&= -\cos \frac{\pi}{4}\\
&= -\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}
\end{align*}
However,
\begin{align*}
\cos \frac{5\pi}{4} &= \cos \left(2\pi - \frac{3\pi}{4}\right)\\
&= \cos \left(\frac{3\pi}{4}\right)\\
&= -\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}
\end{align*}
In both cases, the answer is $-\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}$.
